    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b6150d79-5b21-512a-9500-141e1db1049b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)-\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)\\right)^2\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$. Expand the square and combine the squared terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin x-\\cos x)^2 &=\\sin^2 x-2\\sin x\\cos x+\\cos^2 x \\\\ &=1-2\\sin x\\cos x \\\\ &=1-\\sin(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$. Multiply both terms by $-\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$. The result is\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}\\right) .\n$$",
